    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Normal faults initiate and grow through hierarchical formation of pressure solution structures and their subsequent shearing in Cretaceous carbonates in the leading thrust front of Maiella Mountain, Italy. Through mapping in the field, we document the detailed architecture of faults with increasing slip values from a few mm to ~50 meters and identify pre- existing structural elements and four stages of fault development, with each stage representing addition of a new structural element. The result is a conceptual model that begins with pre-tilting structures (bed-parallel and perpendicular solution surfaces) that are reactivated in shear upon tilting of the beds at the frontal limb of the Maiella anticline. Slip on mechanical layer boundaries and bed- perpendicular solution surfaces results in oblique solution surfaces, linkage of solution surfaces, and fragmentation of rock. Zones of fragmented rock in adjacent mechanical layers link to form a continuous breccia and facilitate fault growth. Ironically these normal faults form through mechanical processes strictly in a compressional regime.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
